[claude] 🔧 mcp__kernelgen__finalize_round: {"conclusion": {"round_num": 1, "expectation_status": "baseline", "root_cause": "Baseline establishes that the seed's quotient->truncate->multiply->subtract arithmetic reproduces the fixed NPU fmod reference exactly (all 18 correctness cases PASSED with abs_err/rel_err 0, matched_ratio 1.0) and that two independent gaps limit throughput: (1) 9 of 12 timing workloads sit on a per-call floor of 15.9-18.7 us versus 10.1-11.6 us for the reference, and a target microbenchmark shows this floor is host/launch bound (n=1024 fp16: full run 15.1 us, the same cached raw launch with the Python body bypassed 10.6 us, reference fmod_ 6.3 us, standard Triton JIT launch 50 us), so the Python bookkeeping above the launch costs ~4.4 us per call; (2) the large 16-bit cases are arithmetic bound, not bandwidth bound (fp16 4096x4096 moves 100.7 MB in 162.7 us = 0.62 TB/s while fp32 moves 201.3 MB in 190.4 us = 1.06 TB/s, i.e. equal per-element cost, and an fp16/bf16-native arithmetic variant measured 164 us versus 163 us for the fp32-widened one, so narrowing the arithmetic domain gives no speedup).", "perf_gap_analysis": "Baseline only; no prior measurement to compare. Observed geo_mean 0.6565 with min_speedup 0.508 on fmod_tensor_-timing-3. Host-bound small workloads score 0.58-0.72 and the large fp32 case is already at parity (0.9477, 1.058 TB/s achieved), so the aggregated gap decomposes into a fixable host-path excess of ~4.4 us per call plus an instruction-bound deficit on 16-bit large tensors where the reference reaches 1.21 TB/s and the candidate 0.62 TB/s.", "next_suggestion": "Shrink only the host path (pre-bind the launch tuple with its grid, resolve the raw-stream getter once but still query the live stream per call, single dict hit, A.get_device() instead of a device object) and keep the kernel byte-identical, targeting the nine host-bound workloads; attack the 16-bit large-tensor instruction count in a later round.", "optimization_level": "host_side_special", "debug_lesson": "On this target the standard Triton JIT launch costs ~50 us per call, so the cached raw-launch path is mandatory; the reference's whole fmod_ call is 6.3 us, so small-tensor speedup is decided by host bookkeeping. msprof profile collection fails server-side on this backend right now (no OpProf marker), so per-call host/device splits must be obtained with event-vs-loop timing instead.", "strategy_evolution": "Start from a validated-correctness seed, quantify the host floor with a target microbenchmark, then optimize the dominant term first instead of guessing at the device kernel.", "knowledge_assessments": []}}
